    layoutKey: "property-first",
    mainDescription:
      "<p>Retail real estate covers a wide range of property types, from a single tenant pad site to a fully anchored neighborhood shopping center, and each type carries a different risk and management profile that matters when a San Jose investor is choosing a replacement property under time pressure. Our retail property lists service provides curated shopping centers, single tenant retail, strip centers, and retail outparcels for San Jose investors completing a 1031 exchange, sourced nationwide and filtered against the investor's actual investment criteria rather than delivered as an unfiltered feed of everything currently on the market.</p>\n\n<h3>How Retail Property Types Differ for Exchange Purposes</h3>\n<p>A multi tenant shopping center generates income from several tenants at once, which spreads vacancy risk across the roster but also requires ongoing common area maintenance, anchor tenant lease negotiations, and periodic re-tenanting work that some San Jose investors are specifically exchanging out of a management intensive property to avoid. Single tenant retail, by contrast, generally carries a net or triple net lease structure where the tenant covers most operating expenses, which trades diversification within a single property for a simpler, more passive ownership experience, provided the single tenant's credit and lease term are strong. Strip centers and unanchored retail sit between these extremes, typically with a handful of local or regional tenants and shorter average lease terms than a national single tenant deal. We ask investors directly whether their exchange goal is passive income, continued active management, or something in between, since that answer changes which retail subtype we prioritize in the search.</p>\n\n<h3>Verifying Retail Property Financials and Tenant Quality</h3>\n<p>We request current rent rolls, lease abstracts, operating statements, and available tax records from listing brokers for every candidate, and where county assessor data is available we cross reference reported income against comparable properties to confirm the numbers hold up rather than relying solely on a broker's marketing package. For multi tenant properties, we review the tenant roster for lease expiration concentration, since several leases expiring in the same year or two creates re-leasing risk that a rent roll alone does not always make obvious. For single tenant properties, we confirm the tenant's credit rating and whether the lease is corporate guaranteed or backed only by a local franchisee, since that distinction affects both the property's risk profile and its resale value down the line. Retail is like kind real estate to industrial, office, multifamily, and every other category of commercial real estate under Section 1031, so investors exchanging out of a San Jose office or industrial property into retail, or the reverse, are making a common and fully qualifying transition.</p>\n\n<p>Because retail acquisitions can range from a straightforward single tenant purchase to a more complex multi tenant transaction requiring estoppel certificates from every tenant, we build the identification and closing timeline around the specific complexity of the property type an investor is pursuing, so the one hundred eighty day acquisition deadline is not put at risk by a due diligence process that was underestimated at the outset. We also model each candidate's purchase price and expected financing against the San Jose relinquished property's sale price and debt payoff to confirm the exchange can close without unintended boot, since California taxes any recognized gain as ordinary income rather than at a preferential capital gains rate, which raises the stakes of an incomplete exchange for Santa Clara County sellers.</p>\n\n<p>We also help San Jose investors think through co-tenancy risk in multi tenant retail, meaning lease clauses that allow a tenant to reduce rent or terminate its lease if an anchor tenant or a minimum percentage of the center's square footage becomes vacant. These clauses are common in shopping center leases and can turn a single anchor vacancy into a cascading rent reduction across the property, so we review co-tenancy language specifically for any multi tenant retail candidate before it is added to an investor's shortlist.</p>\n\n<p>We also review each candidate's parking ratio and site circulation, since retail tenants, particularly quick service restaurants and drive thru concepts, have specific parking and traffic flow requirements that an older retail building may not meet without site modifications. A property that cannot support a tenant's operational requirements without costly site work is a weaker replacement candidate even if the purchase price and cap rate otherwise look attractive.</p>\n\n<p>This is educational information about retail property identification and is not tax, legal, or investment advice. Every investor should confirm property qualification, financing terms, and tax consequences with a Qualified Intermediary and a tax advisor before identifying replacement property.</p>",
